WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:05.900
We have seen loops that include conditional
expressions, such as an if statement, and

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.710
how to use an if statement to break or skip
over part of a loop.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:15.400
Let’s take this concept one step further
and see a loop that includes a compound conditional

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.900
statement, which is a statement with two or
more conditions.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.550
To demonstrate this use of a compound conditional
statement, we will modify the code example

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.000
we used for the continue statement.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.960
In this version of the file, 227-compound,
we still have four coins in a coins tuple

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.940
on line 1, and we still have a for loop that
starts on line 3.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:42.950
Within the loop, each coin is examined, and
if the coin is platinum, a player is moved

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.460
to the next level.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:51.160
However, on line 2, we have a new Boolean
variable for a scepter, and the test value

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.570
is set to True.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:59.170
In the game flow, one must have the scepter
and the platinum coin to move to the next

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.579
level.

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:04.049
We could add another if statement to accommodate
this need.

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:10.119
Still, a more efficient idea would be to add
this criterion to the existing if statement

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:13.869
and make that if statement a compound conditional
statement.

00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:26.049
I will add, to the if statement on line 5,
and that scepter equals True.

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:32.820
This code should return the special message
for the platinum coin since the scepter variable

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.950
is set to True.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:43.259
I will run this code, and we see the special
print statement for the platinum coin.

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:52.850
If, on line 2, I change the scepter variable
to False and then rerun the code, the special

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:59.810
statement for the platinum coin does not print
because both conditions in the if statement

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:03.229
with the and operator are not true.

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:08.429
Using a compound conditional operator saves
time as it prevents one from having to run

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:11.980
multiple if statements to get the same results
from code.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:20.260
We have reached the end of this topic, so
it’s time for another code analysis.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.680
Take a look at this 227-analyze code.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:30.700
